/strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.25), about +3.1\u00b0F since 1873. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.78\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.45 to +1.10) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.64\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.30 to +0.98). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none; 9 months fell. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Montgomery's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.01 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 51.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1929 (78.25 in); the driest, 1954 (26.82 in). Montgomery almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.4 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}